1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,443
This is ECE 358, video 4 b. We solve the long Chapter 1 question of the question bank, question 52. It uses delay, throughput, queues, and encapsulation together.

2
00:00:14,893 --> 00:00:20,631
After this video, you can calculate the transmission and propagation delay on each link.

3
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:25,455
You can find the bottleneck throughput and the router with the longest queue.

4
00:00:25,905 --> 00:00:30,086
You can find when B receives the first frame and a later frame.

5
00:00:30,536 --> 00:00:36,829
And you can find which frames a router with a full memory drops, and the message bytes in a frame.

6
00:00:37,279 --> 00:00:40,629
The question has eight parts. We solve them in order.

7
00:00:41,079 --> 00:00:51,617
Now the long question, question 52 of the question bank. Hosts A and B are connected by four routers, R1 to R4, and five links.

8
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:58,681
The first link, A to R1, is 500 meters long, at 100 Mbps.

9
00:00:59,131 --> 00:01:06,832
The links R1 to R2 and R3 to R4 are 10 kilometers long, at 1 Gbps.

10
00:01:07,282 --> 00:01:14,685
The middle link, R2 to R3, is 10000 kilometers long, also at 1 Gbps.

11
00:01:15,135 --> 00:01:21,897
The last link, R4 to B, is 500 meters long, at only 10 Mbps.

12
00:01:22,347 --> 00:01:26,806
The signal speed is 2 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second.

13
00:01:27,256 --> 00:01:33,912
The transmission rate of each interface equals the rate of its link. The processing delay is 0.

14
00:01:34,362 --> 00:01:41,039
Each frame is 1000 bytes. 1 byte is 8 bits, so a frame is 8000 bits.

15
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:48,529
For parts 1 to 6, host A sends frames as fast as it can, and the routers have infinite buffers.

16
00:01:48,979 --> 00:01:56,937
The question suggests a timing diagram: distance across, time down. We use it for parts 5, 6 and 7.

17
00:01:57,387 --> 00:02:08,651
Part 1 asks how long host A takes to transmit one frame. Part 2 asks for the propagation time from R2 to R3. Pause the video and try it.

18
00:02:09,101 --> 00:02:18,338
Part 1 is a transmission delay. A transmits on its own link, at 100 Mbps. d trans is L over R.

19
00:02:18,788 --> 00:02:24,356
L is one frame: 1000 bytes times 8, which is 8000 bits.

20
00:02:24,806 --> 00:02:31,974
R is 100 Mbps, which is 100 times 10 to the power 6 bits per second.

21
00:02:32,424 --> 00:02:42,856
8000 divided by 100 times 10 to the power 6 is 80 times 10 to the power minus 6 seconds. That is 80 microseconds.

22
00:02:43,306 --> 00:02:49,706
Part 2 is a propagation delay. d prop is the distance d over the signal speed s.

23
00:02:50,156 --> 00:02:59,436
d is 10000 kilometers. That is 10 to the power 4, times 10 to the power 3, which is 10 to the power 7 meters.

24
00:02:59,886 --> 00:03:03,769
s is 2 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second.

25
00:03:04,219 --> 00:03:13,669
10 to the power 7, divided by 2 times 10 to the power 8, is 0.05 seconds. That is 50 milliseconds.

26
00:03:14,119 --> 00:03:17,149
The link rate does not matter for propagation.

27
00:03:17,599 --> 00:03:26,132
Part 3 asks for the average throughput at B, and the instantaneous throughput during one frame. Pause the video and try it.

28
00:03:26,582 --> 00:03:32,790
The throughput of a path is the rate of its bottleneck link. That is the smallest rate on the path.

29
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:40,728
The rates are 100 Mbps, three times 1 Gbps, and 10 Mbps.

30
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:45,829
The smallest is 10 Mbps, on the link R4 to B.

31
00:03:46,279 --> 00:03:50,140
So the average throughput at B is 10 Mbps.

32
00:03:50,590 --> 00:03:57,737
While B receives one frame, the bits arrive over the link R4 to B, at 10 Mbps.

33
00:03:58,187 --> 00:04:07,765
A sends without a pause, so R4 always has a frame to send. So the instantaneous throughput is also 10 Mbps.

34
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:13,271
Part 4 asks which router has the longest queue. Pause the video and try it.

35
00:04:13,721 --> 00:04:20,313
A sends frames back to back. From part 1, it sends one frame every 80 microseconds.

36
00:04:20,763 --> 00:04:32,518
R1, R2 and R3 send at 1 Gbps. 8000 bits at 10 to the power 9 bits per second is 8 microseconds per frame.

37
00:04:32,968 --> 00:04:37,576
So they finish each frame before the next one arrives. They have no queue.

38
00:04:38,026 --> 00:04:48,863
R4 sends on the 10 Mbps link. 8000 bits at 10 to the power 7 bits per second is 800 microseconds per frame.

39
00:04:49,313 --> 00:04:56,759
So the arrival rate, 100 Mbps, is more than the output rate, 10 Mbps.

40
00:04:57,209 --> 00:05:07,001
In each 800 microseconds, 800 divided by 80, which is 10 frames, arrive at R4. Only 1 frame leaves.

41
00:05:07,451 --> 00:05:16,432
So the queue grows by 10 minus 1, which is 9 frames every 800 microseconds. R4 has the longest queue.

42
00:05:16,882 --> 00:05:36,231
Part 5. A starts the first frame at time 0. When does B have the complete frame? Pause the video and try it. Each router uses store-and-forward. It receives the whole frame, then transmits it. The processing delay is 0, and the first frame never waits in a queue.

43
00:05:36,681 --> 00:05:41,929
Link A to R1. A transmits for 80 microseconds, from part 1.

44
00:05:42,379 --> 00:05:51,574
The last bit then propagates 500 meters. 500 over 2 times 10 to the power 8 is 2.5 microseconds.

45
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:59,299
So R1 has the whole frame at 80 plus 2.5, which is 82.5 microseconds.

46
00:05:59,749 --> 00:06:10,330
Link R1 to R2. R1 transmits at 1 Gbps: 8000 over 10 to the power 9 is 8 microseconds.

47
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:19,228
Propagation over 10 kilometers: 10 to the power 4 over 2 times 10 to the power 8 is 50 microseconds.

48
00:06:19,678 --> 00:06:28,147
R2 has the frame at 82.5 plus 8 plus 50, which is 140.5 microseconds.

49
00:06:28,597 --> 00:06:36,832
Link R2 to R3. The rate is 1 Gbps again, so the transmission takes 8 microseconds.

50
00:06:37,282 --> 00:06:43,447
Propagation is 50 milliseconds, from part 2. That is 50000 microseconds.

51
00:06:43,897 --> 00:06:54,884
R3 has the frame at 140.5 plus 8 plus 50000, which is 50148.5 microseconds.

52
00:06:55,334 --> 00:07:03,761
Link R3 to R4 is the same as link R1 to R2: 8 microseconds and 50 microseconds.

53
00:07:04,211 --> 00:07:15,475
R4 has the frame at 50148.5 plus 8 plus 50, which is 50206.5 microseconds.

54
00:07:15,925 --> 00:07:26,399
Link R4 to B. R4 transmits at 10 Mbps: 8000 over 10 to the power 7 is 800 microseconds.

55
00:07:26,849 --> 00:07:33,633
Then 2.5 microseconds of propagation over 500 meters, as on the first link.

56
00:07:34,083 --> 00:07:44,878
B has the whole frame at 50206.5 plus 800 plus 2.5, which is 51009 microseconds.

57
00:07:45,328 --> 00:07:51,173
Check with the sums. All transmission delays add to 904 microseconds.

58
00:07:51,623 --> 00:07:57,362
All propagation delays add to 50105 microseconds.

59
00:07:57,812 --> 00:08:09,951
The total is 904 plus 50105, which is 51009 microseconds, or 51.009 milliseconds.

60
00:08:10,401 --> 00:08:18,081
Here is the formula for part 5. Add the transmission delay and the propagation delay of each of the 5 links.

61
00:08:18,531 --> 00:08:27,171
The transmission delays add to 80 plus 8 plus 8 plus 8 plus 800, which is 904 microseconds.

62
00:08:27,621 --> 00:08:39,333
The propagation delays add to 2.5 plus 50 plus 50000 plus 50 plus 2.5, which is 50105 microseconds.

63
00:08:39,783 --> 00:08:46,951
904 plus 50105 is 51009 microseconds.

64
00:08:47,401 --> 00:08:53,011
Draw the two parts to scale. The transmission part is the thin blue piece at the left.

65
00:08:53,461 --> 00:09:01,525
Propagation is almost all of the time. The link R2 to R3 alone gives 50000 microseconds.

66
00:09:01,975 --> 00:09:10,871
Remember the conditions. This sum is correct only for the first frame. It has no queueing delay, and the processing delay is 0.

67
00:09:11,321 --> 00:09:16,143
Part 6 asks when B has the 4th frame. Pause the video and try it.

68
00:09:16,593 --> 00:09:22,225
Look at R4. Measure the time from the moment the first frame reaches R4.

69
00:09:22,675 --> 00:09:34,173
A sends frames back to back, one every 80 microseconds. The 1 Gbps links do not slow them, because 8 microseconds is less than 80.

70
00:09:34,623 --> 00:09:42,751
So the first four frames reach R4 at 0, 80, 160, and 240 microseconds.

71
00:09:43,201 --> 00:09:47,255
R4 needs 800 microseconds for each frame.

72
00:09:47,705 --> 00:09:58,478
When R4 finishes one frame, the next frame is already waiting, because 80 is less than 800. So R4 sends the four frames back to back.

73
00:09:58,928 --> 00:10:04,069
B has each frame 2.5 microseconds after R4 finishes it.

74
00:10:04,519 --> 00:10:16,743
The 4th frame is complete at 4 times 800 plus 2.5, which is 3202.5 microseconds after the first frame reached R4.

75
00:10:17,193 --> 00:10:30,975
So after the first frame, B gets one frame every 800 microseconds. The 4th frame arrives 3 times 800, which is 2400 microseconds, after the first.

76
00:10:31,425 --> 00:10:43,350
51009 plus 2400 is 53409 microseconds, which is 53.409 milliseconds.

77
00:10:43,800 --> 00:11:00,803
Check: the first frame reached R4 at 50206.5 microseconds. 50206.5 plus 3202.5 is also 53409.

78
00:11:01,253 --> 00:11:14,565
Part 7 changes one fact. R4 can hold a total of four IP packets. A numbers its frames from 0. Which frame does R4 drop first? Pause the video and try it.

79
00:11:15,015 --> 00:11:21,479
Use time after frame 0 reaches R4. Frames arrive every 80 microseconds.

80
00:11:21,929 --> 00:11:29,502
Frame 0 arrives at time 0, and R4 starts to send it. It takes 800 microseconds.

81
00:11:29,952 --> 00:11:36,715
Frames 1, 2 and 3 arrive at 80, 160 and 240 microseconds.

82
00:11:37,165 --> 00:11:45,314
Now R4 holds 4 frames: frame 0, which it is sending, and frames 1, 2 and 3. The memory is full.

83
00:11:45,764 --> 00:11:58,393
Frame 4 arrives at 4 times 80, which is 320 microseconds. There is no space. So R4 drops frame 4. The answer to part a is frame 4.

84
00:11:58,843 --> 00:12:08,315
Frames 5 to 9 arrive at 400 to 720 microseconds. The memory is still full, so R4 drops them too.

85
00:12:08,765 --> 00:12:16,552
Part b. After R4 starts to drop, which is the first frame that B receives? Pause the video and try it.

86
00:12:17,002 --> 00:12:24,063
At 800 microseconds, R4 finishes frame 0. This frees one place in the memory.

87
00:12:24,513 --> 00:12:32,599
At the same moment, frame 10 arrives, because 10 times 80 is 800. So R4 stores frame 10.

88
00:12:33,049 --> 00:12:44,931
Frames 1, 2 and 3 were already in the memory, and B receives them. The first frame after the dropped frames is frame 10. So the answer to part b is frame 10.

89
00:12:45,381 --> 00:12:54,384
The pattern repeats. Frames 11 to 19 are dropped, and frame 20 is stored at 1600 microseconds.

90
00:12:54,834 --> 00:13:05,159
So 1 frame in 10 passes: frames 10 and 20. This matches the rates: 10 Mbps out of 100 Mbps.

91
00:13:05,609 --> 00:13:19,092
The QB does not give answers, so state your assumptions. We count the frame in transmission as one of the four. And when a frame leaves at the same moment as another arrives, the space is free first.

92
00:13:19,542 --> 00:13:28,993
If the four places do not include the frame in transmission, the first drop is frame 5. Part b is frame 10 in both cases.

93
00:13:29,443 --> 00:13:41,667
The last part, part 8. The headers are 20, 20 and 14 bytes. How many bytes of application message fit in one 1000-byte frame? Pause the video and try it.

94
00:13:42,117 --> 00:13:53,893
Write the frame structure. The frame is the link header, the network header, the transport header, and the message. Together they are 1000 bytes. The bar is drawn to scale.

95
00:13:54,343 --> 00:13:59,228
Add the headers. 14 plus 20 plus 20 is 54 bytes.

96
00:13:59,678 --> 00:14:06,014
The message is the rest. 1000 minus 54 is 946 bytes.

97
00:14:06,464 --> 00:14:11,456
The overhead is 54 out of 1000, which is 5.4 percent.

98
00:14:11,906 --> 00:14:23,042
Here is the recap. One. The transmission delay on each link is L over R. The propagation delay is d over s, and it does not depend on the rate.

99
00:14:23,492 --> 00:14:29,188
Two. The throughput is the rate of the bottleneck link. Here it is 10 Mbps.

100
00:14:29,638 --> 00:14:35,846
Three. A queue grows where the arrival rate is more than the output rate. Here that is R4.

101
00:14:36,296 --> 00:14:53,277
Four. The first frame takes the sum of the transmission and propagation delays of all links, 51009 microseconds. After that, frames arrive one per transmission time of the bottleneck link, 800 microseconds.

102
00:14:53,727 --> 00:15:04,778
Five. When the memory of R4 is full, it drops frames 4 to 9 and stores frame 10. In questions without published answers, state your assumptions.

103
00:15:05,228 --> 00:15:17,857
Six. A 1000-byte frame with 54 bytes of headers carries 946 bytes of message. That is the end of video 4 b, and the end of Chapter 1.
